     releases it. The lock is then passed to a waiting thread, which proceeds.

     The core problem mutexes solve is the race condition. Two threads reading and writing the same variable can
     interleave their operations, producing results that neither thread intended. A mutex serializes access,
     restoring the simple mental model of sequential execution for the protected region.

     Mutexes have two states: locked and unlocked. Beyond that, implementations vary. Some are recursive, letting the
     same thread re-lock without deadlocking. Some track ownership, refusing to let a thread unlock a mutex it never
     locked. Others are plain and fast, trading safety for speed.

     Using mutexes correctly is subtle. Deadlock arises when two threads each hold one lock and wait for the other.
     Priority inversion occurs when a low-priority thread holds a lock a high-priority thread needs. Lock contention
     can throttle throughput, turning parallel code into serialized code with extra overhead. Roughly, the lock
     protects correctness but may cost performance.

     Best practices keep critical sections short, acquire locks in a consistent global order, and avoid holding a
     lock while calling code that might lock something else. Prefer higher-level abstractions—channels, immutable
     data, atomic operations—when they fit, reserving raw mutexes for cases where sharing is unavoidable.

     In short, a mutex is a small, sharp tool. Used carefully, it makes concurrency safe; used carelessly, it
     introduces deadlocks and bottlenecks that are hard to diagnose.

